WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
All right, let's start today with a weekly&nbsp;
coronavirus update, it does seem increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:13.040
like what we said last week might be going on is&nbsp;
going on, which is that the plateau in the decline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.600
in cases and deaths that we saw last week was not&nbsp;
really a plateau. It wasn't really a reversal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:26.480
It was an artifact of the storms in much&nbsp;
of the country which slowed down testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:32.080
and vaccination. I'll explain in more detail,&nbsp;
but let's just jump right into the numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:40.480
starting with cases. Of course, on January 11th,&nbsp;
we saw the average peak of daily cases hit 250000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.720
55000 cases per day, a number that a year&nbsp;
ago would have been inconceivable when Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.720
Fauci said we might get to 100000 cases a day.
So many people saying, no, we won't. And we got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.400
an average of two hundred and fifty five thousand&nbsp;
cases a day as of January 11th. But then we saw a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:04.560
reverse and the numbers started to decline and&nbsp;
we saw an average case. Numbers go below 200000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:12.560
a day, below 150000 a day, below 100000 a day,&nbsp;
down to about 70000 cases a day. And then around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:19.680
February 19th, 20th, the decline appeared to stop.&nbsp;
Case numbers even went up a few days. Now, my idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.960
was this is not a real increase. This is because&nbsp;
of the bad weather in the United States. Why would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:33.840
that be the storms that pushed Texas into blackout&nbsp;
conditions right around mid-February lead to lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.280
than actual numbers being reported for some&nbsp;
time. So the decline we saw in mid-February&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:45.360
was bigger than reality because a lot of testing&nbsp;
was being delayed. At the end of February, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:52.080
testing got caught up, we were getting more cases&nbsp;
reported than the real number of new cases as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:02:00.400
backlog tests were processed. So yesterday was&nbsp;
the day with the fewest cases since October 13th,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.680
right around fifty two thousand cases. And I&nbsp;
believe that that's the real number. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.480
there was a period between January 11th and&nbsp;
yesterday where numbers were artificially low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:14.800
and then a period where numbers were artificially&nbsp;
high. I think that the backlog has been cleared.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.840
And we're now back to sort of the real numbers.
One example, a friend of mine's dad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:28.080
was tested around February 15th.
It took 11 days to get the results that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.240
tested positive. He's doing fine. He's actually&nbsp;
vaccinated and has no symptoms whatsoever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:36.640
That is a positive test that should have shown&nbsp;
up far earlier, but because of the delays that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:42.560
didn't show up until February 28th. So bottom&nbsp;
line cases from the peak on January 11th, down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.520
73 percent. That's a great number. We're closing&nbsp;
in on getting below 50000 a day. That would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:53.760
an important milestone. Now in terms of deaths,&nbsp;
a very similar trajectory. Deaths peaked at about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:59.360
3400 today on January 26th from that point.
Deaths are down 42 percent.&nbsp;

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:05.680
We saw a similar bump, right as the storms&nbsp;
passed and the data normalized, there is no doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:12.160
that 900 deaths a day, which is where we are now,&nbsp;
is outrageous. But if you have outrageous, tragic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:18.240
numbers that are going down, you prefer that to&nbsp;
outrageous, tragic numbers that are going up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.840
Another important statistic that we track&nbsp;
our hospitalizations. Good news there is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:29.440
hospitalizations are down from a peak of more than&nbsp;
one hundred and thirty thousand just after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:36.160
start of twenty twenty one, down from one hundred&nbsp;
and thirty K to about 52000 hospitalizations.&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.000
That's a hospitalization&nbsp;
decline of about 60 percent.&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:46.800
As more and more high risk people get vaccinated,&nbsp;
that number should decline more. Now, the really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.520
exciting news, if there's anything exciting&nbsp;
at all to look at in the midst of horrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:57.280
tragedy that's coming from vaccines, we now&nbsp;
have a third vaccine given in emergency use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:03.840
authorization. This is the Johnson and Johnson&nbsp;
vaccine. It is a one dose vaccine. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.280
there are people who are negative on this vaccine&nbsp;
saying, oh, it's less efficacious than the fizer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.400
and unmodern of vaccines. And there are three&nbsp;
important things to consider there. Number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:20.560
the Johnson and Johnson vaccine, even if&nbsp;
less effective at preventing infections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:26.400
appears to be just as good as the other vaccines&nbsp;
at preventing serious covid and keeping people out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:32.560
of hospitals. All right. So, number one, that's&nbsp;
a very important number to it's only one dose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.960
meaning that given X number of doses, if you have&nbsp;
five million JMJ doses, you can vaccinate five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:44.560
million people rather than just 2.5 million if you&nbsp;
had that same number of Fizer or Moderna doses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:51.520
because it's one dose only. And number three, it's&nbsp;
not actually clear that the JMJ vaccine really is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.240
not as good at preventing infection. Now,&nbsp;
I know the headlines are often Pfizer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:02.480
Moderna. Ninety five percent effective, JNJ&nbsp;
only 65, 70 percent. There are studies these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:09.040
still preprint. So take everything with with some&nbsp;
skepticism now. But there are studies that suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:16.320
that because the JMJ Phase three trials were&nbsp;
tested on the variants that were so worried about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:24.000
including the South Africa variant, that the lower&nbsp;
number, the 65 70 percent, reflects the variance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.520
and that if you were to do the Pfizer and&nbsp;
Moderna trials with the variants, you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:36.480
also be seeing 65, 70 percent. So the point is,&nbsp;
it's not really clear JNJ is less effective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:44.160
get whatever vaccine is available. And we had&nbsp;
a very good three day period this past weekend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:50.160
Friday, Saturday, Sunday, where almost seven&nbsp;
million doses were injected over the three days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:57.200
six point nine million doses, in fact. So we are&nbsp;
now averaging one point eight million doses a day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.880
Joe Biden wanted to do a million a day for&nbsp;
100 days. We might be able to double that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:07.600
We might be able to more than double it.&nbsp;
We could even be getting to 2.5 or three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:16.000
million doses a day regularly soon as there are&nbsp;
massive dumps of vaccine doses coming in March.&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:21.120
Realistically, it it's conceivable that&nbsp;
the problem by the end of this month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:28.000
will be do we have enough vaccine vaccination&nbsp;
sites and vaccinators to inject all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.800
doses that we're going to have? Some locations&nbsp;
probably need to start thinking about ramping up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:38.080
to 24/7 vaccination. That's a good problem&nbsp;
to have, assuming you can solve it. We want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.640
to make sure that we can use all of the doses&nbsp;
that we're getting. So things are looking up.&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:49.120
It's a race against the variance, it's a get&nbsp;
a race against people behaving less carefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

